WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.123
In the second part we learn two things.

2
00:00:03.123 --> 00:00:10.570
First, how to build a fundamental set when the roots are repeated and the order is above two.

3
00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:17.537
Second, an applied question: when does the free motion of a mass on a spring oscillate,

4
00:00:17.537 --> 00:00:22.262
and when does it return to equilibrium without oscillating.

5
00:00:22.512 --> 00:00:29.562
The rule: if a root r zero has multiplicity k, it gives k functions: e to the r zero x,

6
00:00:29.562 --> 00:00:36.936
x times that exponential, and so on up to x to the power k minus one times the exponential.

7
00:00:36.936 --> 00:00:43.500
If a complex pair is repeated, the cosine and sine are multiplied by powers of x.

8
00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:47.390
The number of functions always equals the order.

9
00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:54.673
Example: y triple prime plus y double prime minus y prime minus y equals zero.

10
00:00:54.673 --> 00:01:01.345
The characteristic polynomial is r cubed plus r squared minus r minus one.

11
00:01:01.345 --> 00:01:06.936
Group the terms: r squared times r plus one, minus r plus one.

12
00:01:06.936 --> 00:01:11.534
The result is r plus one squared times r minus one.

13
00:01:11.784 --> 00:01:15.227
The simple root one gives e to the x.

14
00:01:15.227 --> 00:01:22.951
Minus one is a double root and gives e to the minus x and x times e to the minus x.

15
00:01:22.951 --> 00:01:28.163
That is three functions, and the order is three as well.

16
00:01:28.163 --> 00:01:37.934
The general solution is C one e to the x plus, in brackets, C two plus C three x, times e to the minus x.

17
00:01:38.184 --> 00:01:39.778
Now the spring.

18
00:01:39.778 --> 00:01:44.347
Mass m, damping coefficient c, stiffness k.

19
00:01:44.347 --> 00:01:51.998
Newton’s law gives m y double prime plus c y prime plus k y equals zero.

20
00:01:51.998 --> 00:01:58.587
Everything is decided by the sign of c squared minus four m k.

21
00:01:58.587 --> 00:02:03.050
Negative means an underdamped oscillation.

22
00:02:03.050 --> 00:02:06.025
Zero means critical damping.

23
00:02:06.025 --> 00:02:11.126
Positive means overdamping, with no oscillation.

24
00:02:11.376 --> 00:02:19.489
Example: m is one, c is two, k is five; the mass is pulled one metre and released from rest.

25
00:02:19.489 --> 00:02:23.369
The roots are minus one plus or minus two i.

26
00:02:23.369 --> 00:02:28.218
The conditions give A equals one and B equals one half.

27
00:02:28.218 --> 00:02:35.008
The solution is e to the minus t times cosine two t plus one half sine two t.

28
00:02:35.008 --> 00:02:37.742
The quasi-period is pi seconds.

29
00:02:37.992 --> 00:02:44.135
Compare the three cases on one graph, all starting from the same position.

30
00:02:44.135 --> 00:02:48.951
The first curve is underdamped: it oscillates around zero.

31
00:02:48.951 --> 00:02:51.607
The second is critically damped,

32
00:02:51.607 --> 00:02:58.332
with c equal to four and k equal to four: the fastest return without oscillation.

33
00:02:58.332 --> 00:03:03.230
The third is overdamped: a slow return without oscillation.

34
00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:13.588
Question: which damping coefficient gives critical damping for a mass of two kilograms on a spring of stiffness eight?

35
00:03:13.588 --> 00:03:17.529
Recall the formula: c squared equals four m k.

36
00:03:17.529 --> 00:03:20.870
Pause the video and choose your answer.
